            <ArticleTitle>Cardiac sarcoidosis and coronary artery disease: a two-hit mechanism to left ventricular dysfunction (or is it)?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>237-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This report describes a case of cardiac sarcoidosis in a 40-year-old man with minimal risk factors for coronary artery disease who was found to have a critical coronary lesion on angiography performed for declining left ventricular function. The case highlights the diagnostic and therapeutic issues surrounding cardiac sarcoidosis and raises the question of a possible link between sarcoidosis and premature coronary artery disease. It also stresses the importance of ruling out ischemia in any patient with declining left ventricular function.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
